I was not familiar with Tabula until I was reading our ‘Python for Data Analysis’ book. I have yet to actually put it into practice but, the application is enticing to say the least! As we know that PDF are usually “locked-in” system of records and typically are published without further revisions. This typically applies to the formatting of the report as well. So, it seems that this library will become very beneficial for applications built on extracting information without having to re-type everything like you stated Selvi. And to your point, if we can eliminate the human element as much as possible to minimize repeated data entry points, this will help minimize errors and delays in data wrangling processes.
You also made a great point regarding the limitations of this library. The fact that it cannot parse through images seems like a missed opportunity but, it does make sense since there are some slight formatting issues that would be a nightmare to try and figure out. It also suggests how this library is able to extract this information from a PDF instead of some character recognition software. Which brings me to another library suggestion. Although not technically built to parse through PDFs, it can “read” images but, you would need to know what the image is to begin with. The library I’m mentioning is ‘PyAutoGUI’ and it has the ’PyScreeze’ and ‘Pillow’ modules that help take a screenshot of your monitor and locate text or an object on your screen based on what you pass through a variable. So, let’s say I’m looking for a specific icon, I can locate that icon on my screen after telling my program to take a screenshot!
	But I digress. Perhaps there is a way to combine the 2 libraries to overcome this small limitation.
